WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.390
GABE WEISS: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.640
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.580
So if you didn't see
Building IoT Applications

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.250
on Google Cloud, stay tuned,
because here's the rewind.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.820
Building an entire
end-to-end IoT application

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:20.230
can feel overwhelming.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:21.410
What devices should I use?

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:22.610
How do I send my data?

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.640
How do I make sure that
everything's secure?

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.190
Once data gets somewhere, how
do I get it into a Datastore?

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:28.690
And once I have
it in storage, how

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:31.480
do I actually use the data
to do anything useful?

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:32.920
Half the battle
in creating an app

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.294
is just knowing
what technologies

00:00:34.294 --> 00:00:35.696
are right for the job.

00:00:35.696 --> 00:00:37.570
This is a pretty typical
architecture diagram

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.660
for an IoT application built
on Google's Cloud platform.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.500
This diagram even has
a lot going on in it.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:43.999
But don't worry,
it's all right, I'm

00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:45.190
going to walk us through it.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:46.651
On the topic of
devices themselves,

00:00:46.651 --> 00:00:48.150
I'm going to hand
wave a little bit.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.100
Anyone that's done
IoT already knows

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.850
there's a lot of debate
going on right now

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:53.933
around hardware choice,
what sort of communication

00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:56.530
protocol you want to choose,
what data protocol is the best

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:57.460
one to use?

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.320
Most of the time,
some external factor's

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.653
going to make this choice for
you, like you need devices

00:01:01.653 --> 00:01:02.920
on the side of a mountain.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.720
So pick whatever works
for the environment

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.077
that the device lives in.

00:01:06.077 --> 00:01:07.660
And now that you've
got your hardware,

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.659
you need a good way to
keep track of all of them

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:11.360
and connect them to
the Cloud securely.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.939
This is what Cloud IoT
Core is designed to do.

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:16.480
It's a managed service and acts
as a gateway for your devices

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.980
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.740
It handles registration of
devices, securely connecting

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.630
and communicating with
them, and can even

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.730
do things like send
configuration messages back

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.360
down to your devices.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.350
Once your devices are sending
their telemetry up to IoT Core,

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.060
these telemetry
payloads get bridged

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.530
into our event stream manager--

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:33.460
Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.540
Events only stay on
Pub/Sub for seven days,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.440
so we need to move them out of
there into permanent storage.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.720
To do that, we pick
Cloud Functions.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.460
Cloud Functions are
serverless methods

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.500
that can be called
directly with HTTP

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.780
or fired in response
to some other event.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.750
In our case, every device
telemetry message coming in

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.300
fires the function which takes
the data from our hardware

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.040
and pushes it into
our storage option--

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.570
Cloud Firestore.

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.960
Firestore is a NoSQL database
with very low latency,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.990
and for our purposes, a special
sauce, which is that it also

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.970
has a push update mechanism.

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:06.610
So our front end can
subscribe to data updates

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.440
and draw our incoming
telemetry data in real time.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.420
So a quick recap
within the recap.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.470
This setup alone would be useful
in terms of gathering data

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:15.700
for future use.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.592
We've gone from device,
through IoT Core, to Pub/Sub,

00:02:18.592 --> 00:02:20.800
and used a Cloud Function
to pull that telemetry data

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.154
into Cloud Firestore.

00:02:22.154 --> 00:02:23.320
So what about visualization?

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.630
Yeah, let me explain.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:25.510
No, there's too much.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:26.800
Let me sum up.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.780
We built two front
ends-- one in Angular

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:30.226
to show off how
easy it is to use

00:02:30.226 --> 00:02:31.600
our batteries-included
JavaScript

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.480
framework to visualize
real-time streaming data,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.180
and the other using Google
Sheets backed by Apps Script

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.830
to show off you a
raw-time windowed data.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.100
We also created a couple
of administrative tabs.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.920
One enabled us to configure
metadata in Firestore,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.840
and the other hooked
up to IoT Core Admin

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.360
SDK, which allowed us to send
these configurations back

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.401
down to our devices
that we had built.

00:02:51.401 --> 00:02:52.900
Obviously, there's
more than one way

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.550
to build an IoT application.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.800
I've run through just the
one happy path doing it here

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.330
and the products involved.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:00.310
In our session at
Cloud Next, we built

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.637
this entire end-to-end
application while on stage

00:03:02.637 --> 00:03:04.720
as I talked through what
some of the other choices

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.390
are in Google Cloud Platform
and showed off some of the code

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:09.010
that we were writing
while on stage.

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.593
I even ran around the room while
attached to our sensor device

00:03:11.593 --> 00:03:13.120
to get some more
interesting data.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.740
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:15.880
check out the link
in the description

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.255
below, and don't forget to
subscribe to the Cloud YouTube

00:03:18.255 --> 00:03:20.060
channel for more great
Next Rewind content.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:20.893
Thanks for watching.

00:03:20.893 --> 00:03:24.180
